s6-taiclock, s6-sntpclock: send on the connected socket, without an address

Symptom (every macOS release): both clock clients fail every round trip
with "Socket is already connected" (EISCONN) and exit 1 with "unable to
contact ... server", against any server. They are unusable on Darwin.
s6-taiclockd, which never connects its socket, is unaffected.

Cause: each client connect()s its UDP socket to the server
(socket_deadlineconnstamp46_g), then sends every query with
socket_sendnb46_g, which is sendto() naming that same server again. The
peer is held twice: by the socket, and by every send.

POSIX leaves this case to the implementation. For a connectionless socket
with a pre-specified peer, sendto() with a destination either sends to it,
overriding the peer, or fails with EISCONN; EISCONN is listed as "may fail"
(IEEE Std 1003.1-2024, sendto(), DESCRIPTION and ERRORS):
https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/sendto.html

- Linux overrides: udp_sendmsg() in net/ipv4/udp.c (v7.3) uses the given
  address, and EISCONN does not occur in that file. The duplicate is
  harmless there, which is why the code works on Linux.
- XNU fails: udp_output() in bsd/netinet/udp_usrreq.c returns EISCONN for
  any destination once inp_faddr is set, even the connected peer itself:
      if (addr) {
          if (inp->inp_faddr.s_addr != INADDR_ANY) {
              error = EISCONN;
  udp6_output() in bsd/netinet6/udp6_output.c does the same for IPv6. The
  check is present unchanged in xnu-1504.15.3 (10.6), xnu-2422.1.72
  (10.9), xnu-4570.1.46 (10.13) and xnu-12377.121.6, so it concerns every
  Darwin release, not one version.

The connect() must stay: a connected UDP socket accepts datagrams only
from its peer, and these programs discard the source address recvfrom()
reports. The connection is what keeps a third party from answering.

Fix: send with skalibs' timed_write() (skalibs/unix-timed.h), which writes
on the connected socket with no address; the socket alone holds the peer.
A UDP write is atomic (the whole datagram, or -1), so the semantics match
socket_sendnb46_g: EAGAIN retried until the deadline, then ETIMEDOUT, any
other error returned with errno set. The exchange functions lose their
now-unused port parameter. Receiving is unchanged: recvfrom() on a
connected socket is valid on Darwin. On Linux the behaviour is identical,
since the connected peer was the destination anyway.

The defect is at s6-networking master (b5929b9), unchanged since the
initial commit (e0fc822, 2014). It is not submitted upstream: Skarnet does
not accept LLM-generated contributions:
https://github.com/skarnet/s6-networking/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING

MacPorts downstream patch; not submitted to or accepted by Skarnet.
External tests, negative controls, and results:
https://github.com/bjornpagen/macports-ports/tree/a9d0bf9ac5e77e79c576ff13092aa40e4f6cb73f

